      "model": "claude-fable-5-1",
      "question": "I've heard the ratio method can give odd answers when gas levels are low, so I want a second opinion on Transformer 1 at site MAIN. Using the average hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene and acetylene over the available window, run the IEC 60599 ratio classification. Separately, work out the Duval Triangle 1 percentages from methane, ethylene and acetylene, and name the zone they fall in (PD, D1, D2, DT, T1, T2 or T3). Tell me whether the two methods agree. If they disagree, tell me which one you trust more and why, looking at whether the gases are high enough for ratios to mean anything. Then name the single most likely failure mode from our known transformer list. Use averages, not single samples. If the combined methane, ethylene and acetylene is under 10 ppm, say that the Duval result isn't meaningful and rely on the ratio result alone. If methane or ethylene has no data at all, say that neither method can be applied reliably and give a key-gas view from the largest gas instead.",
      "answer": "The IEC ratio interpretation tool returned \"LLM unavailable\", so I computed both classifications directly from the averaged readings (5 samples, 2024‑01‑15 → 2024‑01‑19). No further data is needed — all five gases have full coverage.\n\n**Averages (ppm):** H₂ 2869 · CH₄ 7331.2 · C₂H₆ 5457.4 · C₂H₄ 16626.8 · C₂H₂ 7.0\n\n**1. IEC 60599 ratio classification**\n- C₂H₂/C₂H₄ = 7 / 16626.8 = **0.0004** (< 0.1)\n- CH₄/H₂ = 7331.2 / 2869 = **2.56** (> 1)\n- C₂H₄/C₂H₆ = 16626.8 / 5457.4 = **3.05** (1–4)\n- Result: **T2 — thermal fault, 300–700 °C**. Note the third ratio (3.05) sits close to the T3 boundary (> 4), so T2 is a borderline call; a Rogers‑code reading (0‑2‑2) would already tip to T3.\n\n**2. Duval Triangle 1**\n- Sum CH₄ + C₂H₄ + C₂H₂ = 23,965 ppm (far above the 10 ppm meaningfulness threshold)\n- %CH₄ = **30.6 %**, %C₂H₄ = **69.4 %**, %C₂H₂ = **0.03 %**\n- Zone: **T3 — thermal fault > 700 °C** (C₂H₄ > 50 %, C₂H₂ < 15 %)\n\n**3. Do they agree?**\nPartially. Both say a purely thermal fault with essentially no discharge/arcing (acetylene negligible), but they land in adjacent temperature bands: IEC → T2, Duval → T3.\n\n**Which I trust more:** Duval T3. Gas levels are enormous (thousands of ppm, orders of magnitude above IEC 60599 typical 90 % values), so the ratios are numerically valid — the \"low‑gas ratio instability\" concern does not apply here. The disagreement instead comes from the IEC C₂H₄/C₂H₆ ratio sitting right on the T2/T3 edge (3.05 vs 4), whereas the Duval point is well inside T3 (69 % ethylene against a 50 % boundary). Ethylene being the dominant key gas is itself the classic signature of a high‑temperature (> 700 °C) hot spot in oil. Supporting evidence: OTI averages 85 °C with the oil‑temperature alarm and trip flags active.\n\n**4. Most likely failure mode (from the known transformer list):**\n**Thermal Fault — Core/Winding** — a > 700 °C hot spot (e.g., circulating currents, core lamination/clamping hot metal, or a winding connection), rather than bulk oil overheating, partial discharge, or arcing.",
      "score": {
